<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Taifa of Molina</h1><p class="st-meta">1012 CE – 1100 CE (approx.) · capital Molina de Aragón</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An obscure frontier lordship at Molina de Aragón, ruled by the Banu Galbun, whose lord appears as El Cid's friend Abengalbón in the Cantar de mio Cid.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Banu Galbun appear to have taken control of the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molina-de-aragon">Molina</a> and its district in the upper Gallo valley around 1012, as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-march-al-andalus">Middle March</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> fragmented after the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba</a>. Little more is known of its beginnings.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molina-de-aragon">Molina</a> was a small border principality between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">taifas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albarracin">Albarracín</a>, more a fortified lordship than a full kingdom. It is known mainly from Christian sources and from the epic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantar-de-mio-cid">Cantar de mio Cid</a>, in which its lord Abengalbón is a loyal ally who hosts the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-cid">Cid</a>'s wife and daughters.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molina-de-aragon">Molina</a> came under Almoravid control around 1100. In about 1129 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-i-of-aragon">Alfonso I of Aragon</a> took the town, and it was soon granted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lara">Lara</a> family, becoming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-molina">Lordship of Molina</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/taifa-of-molina.jpg" alt="Taifa of Molina" width="320" height="204"><figcaption>Image: Pedro María Reyes Vizcaíno · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molina_aragon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1012 CE – 1100 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Molina de Aragón</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ibn Galbun</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Andalusi Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Banu Galbun</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1056&amp;focus=taifa-of-molina" aria-label="Taifa of Molina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4634 719 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Taifa of Molina lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4954 975L4979 949M4954 975L4954 975"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4979 949h0M4954 975h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4954 975h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4954 975h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Molina de Aragón. <a href="/atlas?year=1056&amp;focus=taifa-of-molina">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
